nteger, m must be rational. Let's try to find any line that passes through three points of T_4.\n\nList all points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4); (2,1),(2,2),(2,3); (3,1),(3,2); (4,1). Let's check possible triples.\n\n- Points with same column: vertical line, not allowed. So any line with three points must have distinct x coordinates.\n\n- Points with same row: horizontal line, not allowed.\n\n- Points with slope -1: points where x+y constant. For T_4, points with x+y = constant: sum = 2: (1,1); sum=3: (1,2),(2,1); sum=4: (1,3),(2,2),(3,1); sum=5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). So each sum line has up to 4 points, but slope -1 lines are not allowed. So we cannot use those.\n\nThus any allowed line must have slope not 0,∞,-1. Let's try slope 1: points where y - x = constant. For T_4, points with y - x = d: d = 0: (1,1),(2,2) (both in T_4). d = 1: (1,2),(2,3) (both in T_4). d = 2: (1,3) (only). d = 3: (1,4) (only). So slope 1 lines can have at most 2 points: (1,1)-(2,2) and (1,2)-(2,3). So at most 2 points.\n\nSlope 2: points where y - 2x = constant. Let's compute: For each point (x,y), compute y - 2x. (1,1): -1; (1,2): 0; (1,3): 1; (1,4): 2; (2,1): -3; (2,2): -2; (2,3): -1; (3,1): -5; (3,2): -4; (4,1): -7. So any constant appears at most twice? Let's see: constant -1 appears for (1,1) and (2,3). So line slope 2 through (1,1) and (2,3) works. That's two points. Constant 0 appears only (1,2). Constant 1 appears only (1,3). Constant 2 appears only (1,4). Constant -2 appears only (2,2). Constant -3 appears only (2,1). Constant -4 appears only (3,2). Constant -5 appears only (3,1). Constant -7 appears only (4,1). So slope 2 lines can have at most 2 points.\n\nSlope 1/2: points where 2y - x = constant. Compute 2y - x: (1,1): 1; (1,2): 3; (1,3): 5; (1,4): 7; (2,1): 0; (2,2): 2; (2,3): 4; (3,1): -1; (3,2): 1; (4,1): -2. So constant 1 appears for (1,1) and (3,2). So line slope 1/2 through (1,1) and (3,2). That's two points. Constant 3 appears only (1,2). Constant 5 only (1,3). Constant 